.2306328", "type": "journal-article", "created": {"date-parts": [[2014, 2, 14]], "date-time": "2014-02-14T14:10:25Z", "timestamp": 1392387025000}, "is-referenced-by-count": 5391, "title": ["Internet of Things for Smart Cities"], "author": [{"given": "Andrea", "family": "Zanella", "sequence": "first", "affiliation": [], "role": [{"vocabulary": "crossref", "role": "author"}]}, {"given": "Nicola", "family": "Bui", "sequence": "additional", "affiliation": [], "role": [{"vocabulary": "crossref", "role": "author"}]}, {"given": "Angelo", "family": "Castellani", "sequence": "additional", "affiliation": [], "role": [{"vocabulary": "crossref", "role": "author"}]}, {"given": "Lorenzo", "family": "Vangelista", "sequence": "additional", "affiliation": [], "role": [{"vocabulary": "crossref", "role": "author"}]}, {"given": "Michele", "family": "Zorzi", "sequence": "additional", "affiliation": [], "role": [{"vocabulary": "crossref", "role": "author"}]}], "container-title": ["IEEE Internet of Things Journal"], "issued": {"date-parts": [[2014, 2]]}, "published": {"date-parts": [[2014, 2]]}}, {"abstract": "<jats:p>\n            Boron-doped silicon nanowires (SiNWs) were used to create highly sensitive, real-time electrically based sensors for biological and chemical species. Amine- and oxide-functionalized SiNWs exhibit pH-dependent conductance that was linear over a large dynamic range and could be understood in terms of the change in surface charge during protonation and deprotonation. Biotin-modified SiNWs were used to detect streptavidin down to at least a picomolar concentration range. In addition, antigen-functionalized SiNWs show reversible antibody binding and concentration-dependent detection in real time. Lastly, detection of the reversible binding of the metabolic indicator Ca\n            <jats:sup>2+</jats:sup>\n            was demonstrated. The small size and capability of these semiconductor nanowires for sensitive, label-free, real-time detection of a wide range of chemical and biological species could be exploited in array-based screening and in vivo diagnostics.\n          </jats:p>", "DOI": "10.1126/science.1062711", "type": "journal-article", "created": {"date-parts": [[2002, 7, 27]], "date-time": "2002-07-27T09:39:52Z", "timestamp": 1027762792000}, "is-referenced-by-count": 5355, "title": ["Nanowire Nanosensors for Highly Sensitive and Selective Detection of Biological and Chemical Species"], "author": [{"given": "Yi", "family": "Cui", "sequence": "first", "affiliation": [{"name": "Department of Chemistry and Chemical Biology, Harvard University, Cambridge, MA 02138, USA."}], "role": [{"vocabulary": "crossref", "role": "author"}]}, {"given": "Qingqiao", "family": "Wei", "sequence": "additional", "affiliation": [{"name": "Department of Chemistry and Chemical Biology, Harvard University, Cambridge, MA 02138, USA."}], "role": [{"vocabulary": "crossref", "role": "author"}]}, {"given": "Hongkun", "family": "Park", "sequence": "additional", "affiliation": [{"name": "Department of Chemistry and Chemical Biology, Harvard University, Cambridge, MA 02138, USA."}], "role": [{"vocabulary": "crossref", "role": "author"}]}, {"given": "Charles M.", "family": "Lieber", "sequence": "additional", "affiliation": [{"name": "Department of Chemistry and Chemical Biology, Harvard University, Cambridge, MA 02138, USA."}, {"name": "Division of Engineering and Applied Sciences, Harvard University, Cambridge, MA 02138 USA."}], "role": [{"vocabulary": "crossref", "role": "author"}]}], "container-title": ["Science"], "issued": {"date-parts": [[2001, 8, 17]]}, "published": {"date-parts": [[2001, 8, 17]]}}, {"DOI": "10.1109/iccv.1998.710815", "title": ["Bilateral filtering for gray and color images"], "container-title": ["Sixth International Conference on Computer Vision (IEEE Cat. No.98CH36271)"], "author": [{"given": "C.", "family": "Tomasi", "sequence": "first", "affiliation": [], "role": [{"vocabulary": "crossref", "role": "author"}]}, {"given": "R.", "family": "Manduchi", "sequence": "additional", "affiliation": [], "role": [{"vocabulary": "crossref", "role": "author"}]}], "issued": {"date-parts": [[null]]}, "created": {"date-parts": [[2002, 11, 27]], "date-time": "2002-11-27T15:42:54Z", "timestamp": 1038411774000}, "is-referenced-by-count": 5300, "type": "proceedings-article"}, {"abstract": "<jats:title>Abstract</jats:title>\n                  <jats:p>This work sets out the author's conclusions in the field to which he directed his main efforts for at least the last ten years of his life. Starting from an examination of his already well-known distinction between performative utterances and statements, he finally abandons that distinction, replacing it with a more general theory of ‘illocutionary forces’ of utterances, which has important bearings on a wide variety of philosophical problems.</jats:p>", "DOI": "10.1093/acprof:oso/9780198245537.001.0001", "type": "book", "created": {"date-parts": [[2011, 10, 11]], "date-time": "2011-10-11T15:04:12Z", "timestamp": 1318345452000}, "is-referenced-by-count": 5075, "title": ["How To Do Things With Words"], "author": [{"given": "J.L.", "family": "Austin", "sequence": "first", "affiliation": [], "role": [{"vocabulary": "crossref", "role": "author"}]}], "issued": {"date-parts": [[1975, 9, 18]]}, "published": {"date-parts": [[1975, 9, 18]]}}], "items-per-page": 25, "query": {"start-index": 0, "search-terms": null}}}